<%# This is the full page rendered by AlertRulesController#edit (see
    app/controllers/alert_rules_controller.rb) — the browser sees this
    when visiting GET /alert_rules/:id/edit. The controller has already
    loaded the specific record being edited into the `@alert_rule` instance
    variable (an "instance variable" — starts with @ — is how Rails passes
    data from a controller action into the view template it renders). %>
<div class="mb-6">
  <%# "mb-6" is a Tailwind utility class adding margin below this <div>,
      separating the heading from the form card underneath it. %>
  <%# "text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400" makes this a large, bold
      heading in this app's green accent color. `<%= @alert_rule.name %>`
      prints the record's saved `name` value into the page — the equals
      sign in `<%= %>` means "run this Ruby and insert the result as text
      here," as opposed to plain `<% %>` (no equals) used elsewhere in this
      app for logic that produces no visible output on its own. %>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400">Edit: <%= @alert_rule.name %></h1>
</div>
<%# "card" is this app's custom CSS class giving this <div> a consistent
    boxed/panel look (background, border, padding, etc. — defined in the
    app's stylesheet, not built into Tailwind or Rails). "max-w-2xl" caps
    how wide the box can grow on large screens, keeping the form from
    stretching uncomfortably wide. %>
<div class="card max-w-2xl">
  <%# `render "form", alert_rule: @alert_rule` includes the PARTIAL defined
      in app/views/alert_rules/_form.html.erb at this exact point in the
      page. A "partial" is a reusable snippet of view template whose
      filename starts with an underscore; Rails' `render "form"` call
      strips that underscore convention away and looks for a file named
      "_form.html.erb" in this same alert_rules view folder. The
      `alert_rule: @alert_rule` part hands that partial a LOCAL variable
      named `alert_rule` (no @ — partials receive data explicitly like
      this, rather than automatically seeing this page's instance
      variables) so the shared form fields can be pre-filled with this
      specific record's current values. This exact same partial is also
      used by app/views/alert_rules/new.html.erb, so the create and edit
      forms can never drift out of sync with each other. %>
  <%= render "form", alert_rule: @alert_rule %>
</div>
